### 1.5 Semantic risk that only now becomes reachable (copy vs alias)

`std.ReadOnlyArray<T>` is an abstract over `Array<T>` (`samples/std/ReadOnlyArray.hx`) that "erases before any target sees it"; `docs/specs/features/18-immutability.md:49-56` says a container conversion "retains the same container storage" and a retained mutable alias stays visible. On Swift the fixed ops call `toMutableArray()`, which **copies** (`TiqianArray(Array(self))`). Array->ReadOnlyArray is correctly a view (`ReadOnlyArray(operand)`, no copy); ReadOnlyArray->Array is a copy. Because the pre-fix text could not compile, this path has never been exercised by a passing build or test, so its copy-vs-alias semantics are unvalidated. If the cross-target contract for that direction is shared storage, the fix turns a compile error into a silent behavioural divergence. Flagged, not adjudicated (the spec section I read covers the Array->ReadOnlyArray direction explicitly).

