### 2.4 The four guards, checked one by one

**(a) "only functions the emitter keeps reach this point" — field gate: exactly enforced; class gate: an admitted approximation with an unproven "harmless" premise.**

`includeStaticFunc` (Compiler.hx:232-244) is:
```haxe
if (!f.isStatic || f.field.isPublic || f.field.meta.has(":test")) return true;
if (RustDecl.isExceptionSubclass(cls)) return true;
return state.referencedStatics.exists(cls.module + "." + f.field.name);
```
`fieldEmitted` (1421-1425) is the same predicate, same order, same key format. OK Exact mirror.

The class gate is not exact. The emitter (`classDecl`, 143-153) is `if (isSyntheticImpl) {drop unless referencedImpls} else if (!isInterface && isInlineOnly(...)) {drop}` — an **if/else-if**. The scan applies the two tests **sequentially**. So for a referenced synthetic impl (`*_Impl_`), the emitter never applies the inline-only filter while the scan does; and `scanClassInlineOnly` is not `PolicyQueries.isInlineOnly`:
* `PolicyQueries.isInlineOnly` (packages/compiler/PolicyQueries.hx:317) returns `true` only when there are no var fields and all func fields are `MethInline`/`MethMacro`; any var field => `false`.
* `scanClassInlineOnly` counts `FVar` as neither function nor non-inline and then returns `funcs == 0 || allInline`, so a class holding **only variables** returns `true` (scan skips) where `PolicyQueries` returns `false` (emitter keeps).
Both deviations are in the "skip a class the emitter keeps" direction (under-registration), which the code comment's "erring toward registering" claim does not cover. Reachability of the harmful case needs a class that both contains a live rethrow and is dropped by the scan but kept by the emitter; I could not build one from the current corpus (the growth path never fires at all, 2.3), so I report it as a code-level divergence with unverified reachability, not as a demonstrated defect.

The comment also asserts "an unused registration is harmless". That premise is not established by anything I could check: the author's own regression story is that a *spurious* growth variant broke a live `match` (E0004), i.e. spurious growth is precisely the failure mode. Whether it is harmless depends on whether every consumer of the grown enum has a wildcard arm; since no growth variant is emitted anywhere in the corpus, I could not observe the generated matches.

**(b) "the payload enum comes from the thrown class's own constructor" — true for the enum identity; the *name* still comes from a module-keyed map; and last-wins remains inside the constructor.**

`classPayloadEnum` (2513-2530) reads the class's own constructor and takes the **last** `TEnum` argument (`found = {…}` overwrites). So:
* It is genuinely per-class, not `exceptionPayloads`-keyed. OK (This part of the claim holds.)
* With a constructor taking two enum arguments it is last-wins *within the constructor* — the same class of defect, one level down. Constructible: `class FooException extends haxe.Exception { public function new(a:AFault, b:BFault) }` => the scan targets `BFault`. Every exception class in the corpus has exactly one constructor argument and it is a single enum (`ValueException(error:ValueError)`, `VectorException(error:VectorError)`, `UStringException(fault:UStringFault)`, `CoreException(error:CoreFault)`, `JsonException(fault:JsonFault)`, `SemverException(error:SemverFault)`, `HaxeExceptionOps.hx:27 new(message:String)`), so the case is constructible but absent.
* The **registration target name** does come from a module-keyed map: `state.payloadEnumNames.get(payloadEnum.module)` (2492), populated last-writer-wins at 1028-1032. See (c).

**(c) "the derived enum must match the payload name recorded for its module" — enforced as written, but the check is itself the module-keyed map, and its failure mode is a silent skip.**

```haxe
final enumName = state.payloadEnumNames.get(payloadEnum.module);
if (enumName == null || enumName != payloadEnum.name) return;
```
`payloadEnumNames` is keyed by enum module and assigned in a loop over every exception subclass and every enum constructor argument (1024-1033), so it holds one name per module, last writer wins. Two payload enums in one module make the "loser" fail this check. The consequence is not a diagnostic but a `return`: no growth registration, while the emitter still renders the wrapper — its fallback is `errorTypeName + "::" + member.name + "Fault(" + raw + ")"` **without** `Box::new` (RustExpr.hx:2184), which references a variant that was never emitted. Constructible (a module declaring `enum AFault` and `enum BFault` with two exception classes), absent from the corpus (every fault enum has its own module: `registry.CoreFault`, `registry.JsonFault`, `registry.SemverFault`, `boring.NoSuchElementFault`, `std.UStringFault`).

**(d) "a declared variant of the same name is never shadowed by a growth variant" — NOT enforced for names that `toUpperCamelCase` rewrites.**

```haxe
final variant = cls.name + "Fault";
if (enumDeclaresVariant(payloadEnum.module, variant)) return;
```
`enumDeclaresVariant` (2536-2548) compares the **raw Haxe constructor name** (`en.get().constructs.keys()`) against `variant`. But declared variants are emitted as `RustImports.toUpperCamelCase(o.name)` (RustDecl.hx:3023 and 3030), while the growth variant is emitted verbatim (`item.variant`, RustDecl.hx:981). `toUpperCamelCase` (RustImports.hx:227) lower-cases all-upper names and re-cases each `_`-separated part, so any constructor name whose camel-cased form differs from its raw form escapes the check. Concrete counterexample: a fault enum declaring `C_EXCEPTION_FAULT(…)` in a module together with an exception class named `CException` — the declared variant emits as `CExceptionFault`, the growth name is `CExceptionFault`, `enumDeclaresVariant` compares `"C_EXCEPTION_FAULT" != "CExceptionFault"`, misses, and the enum is emitted with two `CExceptionFault` variants => E0428. (Haxe requires enum constructors to start uppercase, so `cExceptionFault` is not legal, but the underscore forms are.) The corpus uses plain upper-camel constructor names (`Missing`, `Config`, `Tree`), so this is constructible but not present. Note also that the check scans **all** enums in the module (broader than the payload enum), which can over-skip: an unrelated enum in the same module declaring `<Class>Fault` suppresses a needed registration.

### 2.5 The scan/emitter key asymmetry (the deepest issue I found)

The scan registers growth on the **thrown class's payload enum** (`enumName` from `payloadEnumNames`), while the emitter looks it up on the **enclosing function's/closure's Result error enum**:

```
RustExpr.hx:2157  if (errorTypeName == null || !StringTools.endsWith(errorTypeName, "Fault")) return raw;
RustExpr.hx:2179  final growth = state.enumGrowthFor(errorTypeName);     // lookup key = function error enum
Compiler.hx:2524  state.registerFaultConversion(enumName, ..., variant); // registration key = class payload enum
```
`errorTypeName` is set per function from the declared `Result` error type (RustExpr.hx:372) and retargeted inside callees/closures (5974, 11570). Nothing in the code enforces that the enclosing function's error enum equals the thrown exception class's payload enum; when they differ, the registration is invisible to the emitter, which then takes the unboxed fallback (`...::<Class>Fault(raw)`) and can reference an undeclared variant. The pre-fix code produced that same fallback, so this is a limitation of the fix's reach rather than a new regression — but it means guards (b)/(c) cannot be validated by construction alone, and the corpus cannot validate them at all because the path never fires.

